WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.418
The Open Window, by Saki.

00:00:02.418 --> 00:00:04.770
“My aunt will be down presently, Mr.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:12.281
Nuttel,” said a very self-possessed young lady of fifteen; “in the meantime you must try and put up with me.”

00:00:12.281 --> 00:00:21.294
Framton Nuttel endeavoured to say the correct something which should duly flatter the niece of the moment without unduly discounting the aunt that was to come.

00:00:21.294 --> 00:00:31.891
Privately he doubted more than ever whether these formal visits on a succession of total strangers would do much towards helping the nerve cure which he was supposed to be undergoing.

00:00:31.891 --> 00:00:44.979
“I know how it will be,” his sister had said when he was preparing to migrate to this rural retreat; “you will bury yourself down there and not speak to a living soul, and your nerves will be worse than ever from moping.

00:00:44.979 --> 00:00:49.550
I shall just give you letters of introduction to all the people I know there.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.637
Some of them, as far as I can remember, were quite nice.”

00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:55.291
Framton wondered whether Mrs.

00:00:55.291 --> 00:01:01.990
Sappleton, the lady to whom he was presenting one of the letters of introduction, came into the nice division.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:09.422
“Do you know many of the people round here?” asked the niece, when she judged that they had had sufficient silent communion.

00:01:09.422 --> 00:01:11.858
“Hardly a soul,” said Framton.

00:01:11.858 --> 00:01:20.609
“My sister was staying here, at the rectory, you know, some four years ago, and she gave me letters of introduction to some of the people here.”

00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:24.171
He made the last statement in a tone of distinct regret.

00:01:24.171 --> 00:01:29.710
“Then you know practically nothing about my aunt?” pursued the self-possessed young lady.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.955
“Only her name and address,” admitted the caller.

00:01:32.955 --> 00:01:34.514
He was wondering whether Mrs.

00:01:34.514 --> 00:01:37.870
Sappleton was in the married or widowed state.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:42.954
An undefinable something about the room seemed to suggest masculine habitation.

00:01:42.954 --> 00:01:49.694
“Her great tragedy happened just three years ago,” said the child; “that would be since your sister’s time.”

00:01:49.694 --> 00:01:57.233
“Her tragedy?” asked Framton; somehow in this restful country spot tragedies seemed out of place.

00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:06.716
“You may wonder why we keep that window wide open on an October afternoon,” said the niece, indicating a large French window that opened on to a lawn.

00:02:06.716 --> 00:02:13.756
“It is quite warm for the time of the year,” said Framton; “but has that window got anything to do with the tragedy?”

00:02:13.756 --> 00:02:21.189
“Out through that window, three years ago to a day, her husband and her two young brothers went off for their day’s shooting.

00:02:21.189 --> 00:02:22.525
They never came back.

00:02:22.525 --> 00:02:29.685
In crossing the moor to their favourite snipe-shooting ground they were all three engulfed in a treacherous piece of bog.

00:02:29.685 --> 00:02:37.175
It had been that dreadful wet summer, you know, and places that were safe in other years gave way suddenly without warning.

00:02:37.175 --> 00:02:39.388
Their bodies were never recovered.

00:02:39.388 --> 00:02:41.520
That was the dreadful part of it.”

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.105
Here the child’s voice lost its self-possessed note and became falteringly human.

00:02:47.105 --> 00:02:57.110
“Poor aunt always thinks that they will come back some day, they and the little brown spaniel that was lost with them, and walk in at that window just as they used to do.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.800
That is why the window is kept open every evening till it is quite dusk.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:17.472
Poor dear aunt, she has often told me how they went out, her husband with his white waterproof coat over his arm, and Ronnie, her youngest brother, singing ‘Bertie, why do you bound?’ as he always did to tease her, because she said it got on her nerves.

00:03:17.472 --> 00:03:26.001
Do you know, sometimes on still, quiet evenings like this, I almost get a creepy feeling that they will all walk in through that window—”

00:03:26.616 --> 00:03:28.777
She broke off with a little shudder.

00:03:28.777 --> 00:03:36.453
It was a relief to Framton when the aunt bustled into the room with a whirl of apologies for being late in making her appearance.

00:03:36.453 --> 00:03:39.595
“I hope Vera has been amusing you?” she said.

00:03:39.595 --> 00:03:42.472
“She has been very interesting,” said Framton.

00:03:42.472 --> 00:03:45.401
“I hope you don’t mind the open window,” said Mrs.

00:03:45.401 --> 00:03:52.560
Sappleton briskly; “my husband and brothers will be home directly from shooting, and they always come in this way.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.562
They’ve been out for snipe in the marshes to-day, so they’ll make a fine mess over my poor carpets.

00:03:58.562 --> 00:04:00.728
So like you men-folk, isn’t it?”

00:04:00.728 --> 00:04:07.717
She rattled on cheerfully about the shooting and the scarcity of birds, and the prospects for duck in the winter.

00:04:07.717 --> 00:04:10.534
To Framton it was all purely horrible.

00:04:10.534 --> 00:04:27.038
He made a desperate but only partially successful effort to turn the talk on to a less ghastly topic; he was conscious that his hostess was giving him only a fragment of her attention, and her eyes were constantly straying past him to the open window and the lawn beyond.

00:04:27.038 --> 00:04:33.522
It was certainly an unfortunate coincidence that he should have paid his visit on this tragic anniversary.

00:04:33.522 --> 00:04:55.315
“The doctors agree in ordering me complete rest, an absence of mental excitement, and avoidance of anything in the nature of violent physical exercise,” announced Framton, who laboured under the tolerably widespread delusion that total strangers and chance acquaintances are hungry for the least detail of one’s ailments and infirmities, their cause and cure.

00:04:55.315 --> 00:04:59.876
“On the matter of diet they are not so much in agreement,” he continued.

00:04:59.876 --> 00:05:01.264
“No?” said Mrs.

00:05:01.264 --> 00:05:06.137
Sappleton, in a voice which only replaced a yawn at the last moment.

00:05:06.137 --> 00:05:11.825
Then she suddenly brightened into alert attention—but not to what Framton was saying.

00:05:11.825 --> 00:05:14.170
“Here they are at last!” she cried.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:18.927
“Just in time for tea, and don’t they look as if they were muddy up to the eyes!”

00:05:18.927 --> 00:05:25.937
Framton shivered slightly and turned towards the niece with a look intended to convey sympathetic comprehension.

00:05:25.937 --> 00:05:31.023
The child was staring out through the open window with dazed horror in her eyes.

00:05:31.023 --> 00:05:37.285
In a chill shock of nameless fear Framton swung round in his seat and looked in the same direction.

00:05:37.285 --> 00:05:50.007
In the deepening twilight three figures were walking across the lawn towards the window; they all carried guns under their arms, and one of them was additionally burdened with a white coat hung over his shoulders.

00:05:50.007 --> 00:05:53.618
A tired brown spaniel kept close at their heels.

00:05:53.618 --> 00:06:02.286
Noiselessly they neared the house, and then a hoarse young voice chanted out of the dusk: “I said, Bertie, why do you bound?”

00:06:02.286 --> 00:06:12.223
Framton grabbed wildly at his stick and hat; the hall-door, the gravel-drive, and the front gate were dimly-noted stages in his headlong retreat.

00:06:12.223 --> 00:06:17.656
A cyclist coming along the road had to run into the hedge to avoid an imminent collision.

00:06:17.656 --> 00:06:26.171
“Here we are, my dear,” said the bearer of the white mackintosh, coming in through the window; “fairly muddy, but most of it’s dry.

00:06:26.171 --> 00:06:28.692
Who was that who bolted out as we came up?”

00:06:28.692 --> 00:06:31.071
“A most extraordinary man, a Mr.

00:06:31.071 --> 00:06:32.324
Nuttel,” said Mrs.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:40.029
Sappleton; “could only talk about his illnesses, and dashed off without a word of good-bye or apology when you arrived.

00:06:40.029 --> 00:06:42.326
One would think he had seen a ghost.”

00:06:42.926 --> 00:06:48.997
“I expect it was the spaniel,” said the niece calmly; “he told me he had a horror of dogs.

00:06:48.997 --> 00:07:02.124
He was once hunted into a cemetery somewhere on the banks of the Ganges by a pack of pariah dogs, and had to spend the night in a newly dug grave with the creatures snarling and grinning and foaming just above him.

00:07:02.124 --> 00:07:04.490
Enough to make anyone lose their nerve.”

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.791
Romance at short notice was her speciality.
